 and genies grant prophetic wishes. *Stranger Magic* examines the wondrous tales of the *Arabian Nights*, their profound impact on the West, and the progressive exoticization of magic since the eighteenth century, when the first European translations appeared. The *Nights* seized European readers' imaginations during the siècle des Lumières, inspiring imitations, spoofs, turqueries, extravaganzas, pantomimes, and mauresque tastes in dress and furniture. Writers from Voltaire to Goethe to Borges, filmmakers from Raoul Walsh on, and countless authors of children's books have adapted its stories. What gives these tales their enduring power to bring pleasure to readers and audiences? Their appeal, Marina Warner suggests, lies in how the stories' magic stimulates the creative activity of the imagination. Their popularity during the Enlightenment was no accident: dreams, projections, and fantasies are essential to making the leap beyond the frontiers of accepted knowledge into new scientific and literary spheres.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Mythology in literature","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-stranger-magic-charmed-states-and-the-arabian-nights-marina-warner"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Tales, history and criticism","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-stranger-magic-charmed-states-and-the-arabian-nights-marina-warner"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Tales, arab countries","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-stranger-magic-charmed-states-and-the-arabian-nights-marina-warner"}],"displaySummary":"The *Nights* seized European readers' imaginations during the siècle des Lumières, inspiring imitations, spoofs, turqueries, extravaganzas, pantomimes, and mauresque tastes in dress and furniture. Writers from Voltaire to Goethe to Borges, filmmakers from Raoul Walsh on, and countless authors of children's books have adapted its st